    prompt: "An interviewer draws a large grid and places a single chess king on square (9, 9). Two players alternate moves. On your turn you must move the king one step closer to the home corner (0, 0): exactly one square left, one square down, or one square diagonally down-left. The king can never move up, right, or stand still. Whoever moves the king onto (0, 0) wins. You move first from (9, 9). The interviewer asks: \"Should you take this game, and what's your plan?\"\n\nYou have thirty seconds before you're allowed to touch the marker. What is the single most useful FIRST move on the whiteboard — the one that turns this into a problem you can actually solve under pressure?",
    correct: "Label the home corner (0, 0) as a LOSS for the player about to move from it, then propagate outward: a square is a LOSS if and only if every legal move lands on a WIN; otherwise it is a WIN. Working backward from the terminal square reveals the structure.",
    wrong: [
      ["Start at (9, 9) and draw out the game tree of legal moves, exploring each branch forward until you can see which opening move wins.", "It feels like the honest, complete approach, and forward simulation is most people's default. But each square has up to three moves, so the tree fans out into hundreds of nodes before any branch terminates, and you will run out of whiteboard and clock before learning anything decisive.", "The game has one square whose value is free; start where the answer is certain, not where the king happens to sit."],
      ["Guess that the answer is a parity rule, write 'winner = player matching the parity of 9 + 9 = 18,' and check that single claim.", "Parity hunches often pay off in these games, so the guess smells right and is fast. But the relevant invariant here is parity of each coordinate separately (both-even squares are the losing ones), not the parity of the coordinate sum, so an unjustified shortcut quietly lands on the wrong invariant.", "Don't assume which invariant governs — let the backward labels reveal the real pattern instead of guessing its form."],
      ["Compute the Sprague-Grundy (nim) value of (9, 9) by recursively mex-ing all reachable squares, then declare a win if it is nonzero.", "It is genuinely correct and sounds impressively rigorous, which is exactly why it tempts a strong candidate. But for a single token with one terminal you only need WIN/LOSS labels, not full Grundy values; the mex machinery is correct-but-overkill and burns your thirty seconds on bookkeeping you do not need.", "You only need to know who wins, not by how much — a plain two-color backward labeling is lighter and gets you there faster."],
    ],
    lesson: "Almost every candidate's hand twitches toward the king's current square, (9, 9), and starts asking \"where can I go from here?\" That is the forward instinct, and on a game like this it is a quiet trap: from (9, 9) there are three replies, each of which branches into three more, and the tree fans out into the hundreds before you learn anything. You will fill the whiteboard with arrows and still not know whether to accept the game. The thirty-second discipline is to notice that there is exactly one square in this whole game whose value you know for free, and it is not the one the king is sitting on.\n\nThe deep idea of backward induction is that a sequential game with a known ending should be read from the ending. There is precisely one terminal fact here: someone who is forced to move when the king is already home has lost, and someone who can move it home this turn has won. Everything else is downstream of that fact. So the productive first mark on the board is not an arrow from (9, 9) — it is a label on (0, 0). Once one square is labeled, the rule for labeling its neighbors writes itself, and the labels flow outward like a tide toward the king.\n\nThe reason this matters for an interview is that the right setup collapses an exponential search into a tiny, mechanical sweep, and it often exposes a pattern long before you reach the starting square. The thing you are racing the clock to find is not a number — it is the orientation of the problem. Get the direction of reasoning right and the arithmetic becomes trivial; get it wrong and no amount of fast arithmetic saves you.",
    solution: "The correct first move is to anchor the one square whose value is certain — the home corner — and let backward induction carry that certainty outward.\n\nSet the convention by looking at the END, not the start. Call a square a LOSS if the player who must move from it will lose under perfect play, and a WIN if that player can force a win. The terminal square (0, 0) is where the game is already over: a player facing the king already home has no winning move to make, so (0, 0) is a LOSS for the mover. (Equivalently: the player who just moved the king home is the winner.) That single labeled square is your seed.\n\nNow the propagation rule writes itself, and it is the heart of backward induction: a square is a WIN if at least one legal move lands on a LOSS (you hand the bad square to your opponent), and a square is a LOSS if every legal move lands on a WIN (whatever you do, you hand your opponent a winning square). Because every move strictly decreases x + y, there are no cycles, so each square's label is fully determined by squares closer to home that you have already labeled. Sweep outward.\n\nDo the first ring by hand. (1, 0), (0, 1) and (1, 1) can each move directly onto (0, 0), a LOSS, so all three are WINs. Now (2, 0): its only legal move is left to (1, 0), a WIN (there is no down move and the diagonal needs y > 0), so (2, 0) is a LOSS. Likewise (0, 2) and (2, 2) come out as LOSSes. Continue the sweep and a crisp pattern emerges: a square is a LOSS exactly when BOTH coordinates are even. (A quick computer sweep confirms it: the losing squares are precisely the (even, even) cells.)\n\nRead off the answer. The king starts at (9, 9) — odd, odd — which is a WIN for the player to move. So YES, take the game: you move first, and your winning plan is to always move the king onto a both-even square, then restore the both-even pattern after every reply. Concretely, from (9, 9) step diagonally to (8, 8); thereafter, whatever your opponent does to break the even-even pattern (they must, since from an even-even square every move makes at least one coordinate odd), you can always restore it, until you finally land on (0, 0) yourself.\n\nThe takeaway for the strategy: the load-bearing decision happened before any computation. The candidate who labels (0, 0) first solves the game in under a minute and even discovers the parity invariant for free. The candidate who starts enumerating moves from (9, 9) drowns, and the candidate who reaches for full Grundy values does correct but unnecessary work. Same problem, same person — only the direction of reasoning differs.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "Two players slide a single token across a grid toward the corner (0, 0); each turn moves it one cell left, down, or diagonally down-left, and the player who places it on (0, 0) wins. The token starts at (9, 9) and it's your move. Of the things you could write down first, which one actually lets you decide whether to play and how — and why is starting from the corner rather than from the token the key?" },
