    lesson: "Here is the shape of the trap before you touch a number. The instinct is to find \"the rate at which the camel loses bananas per kilometre\" and multiply by 1000. But there is no single rate. The camel can only haul 1000 at a time, so to advance a pile bigger than one load you must make multiple forward trips — and every forward trip except the last is paired with a return trip to come back for more, and the camel eats on the way back too. That means the effective cost per kilometre depends entirely on how many loads you are currently dragging along.\n\nSo picture the journey not as a smooth grind but as a staircase. While the pile is between 2000 and 3000 bananas it takes three loads to move, costing a lot per kilometre. The moment the pile drops to exactly 2000 you no longer need that third forward-and-back trip, and the cost per kilometre suddenly falls. Drop to 1000 and it falls again. The whole optimization question becomes: where exactly should the cost step down, and is it ever worth carrying spare capacity past those step points? The answer is a clean exchange argument — you want to spend the expensive high-rate kilometres on as short a stretch as possible, which means stepping down the instant a full load's worth of bananas has been consumed, never a kilometre sooner or later.\n\nRun the staircase honestly and the cheaper later kilometres do most of the distance, while the brutal early kilometres are confined to a short stretch. The arithmetic of \"how far does each step reach before the pile crosses the next load boundary\" is what pins down the final number — and it is a number most people guess too high or too low because they flatten the staircase into a single slope.",
    solution: "Look, here is the one idea I actually want you to walk away with: when something is consumed in proportion to how much work-in-transit you are carrying, the cost-per-step is not constant — it steps down as the load shrinks, and the whole optimization is \"spend the expensive steps over the shortest possible distance.\" That single reframe turns a scary word problem into three short arithmetic lines.\n\nConcretely. The camel carries at most 1000. With 3000 bananas you have three loads. To move that pile forward 1 km you carry a load ahead (eat 1), walk back (eat 1), carry the next load ahead (eat 1), walk back (eat 1), carry the third load ahead (eat 1). That is 3 forward trips and 2 returns = 5 bananas burned per km. This 5/km rate holds only while you genuinely need three forward trips — i.e. while the pile exceeds 2000. So you keep this up until the pile hits exactly 2000, which costs 1000 bananas and covers 1000/5 = 200 km. You are now at the 200 km mark with 2000 bananas.\n\nNow you have two loads. Moving 1 km costs 2 forward + 1 return = 3 bananas/km. You run this until the pile drops to exactly 1000: that burns another 1000 bananas over 1000/3 = 333⅓ km. You are now at 200 + 333⅓ = 533⅓ km with exactly 1000 bananas — a single load. From here there is no reason to return for anything, so you simply walk the rest: 1000 − 533⅓ = 466⅔ km remain, costing 466⅔ bananas. You arrive with 1000 − 466⅔ = 533⅓ bananas. That is the answer, and option A names the rule that produced it: switch cost tiers exactly when the pile crosses a multiple of the carry capacity.\n\nWhy the switch points are forced (the exchange-argument heart): suppose you switched down from the 5/km tier early, before the pile reached 2000. Then on some stretch you'd be carrying less than a full third load yet still paying for extra trips you didn't need — wasted motion. Switch late, and you're hauling a sliver of a third load that forces a whole extra return trip across kilometres where it isn't earning its keep. Either deviation strictly wastes bananas, so the optimum sheds a tier precisely at each 1000-boundary. The reason I care that you internalize this: in trading and in life you will constantly meet costs that scale with inventory-in-flight — funding a position, carrying risk, paying to move things in batches. The amateur picks one average rate and is confidently wrong; the pro notices the cost is a staircase and asks where each step falls. Slower and right beats fast and wrong. That habit — \"is this cost constant, or does it step with the load?\" — is the whole gift.",
